SCHEMA-HASH-DECODE — PROBED 2026-10-16, BEFORE BUILDING. One finding changes the plan.

CHECK THESE WITH `--draft`: several of the five probes carry DELIBERATELY UNUSED warm-up
steps, whose only job is to bump the proof's fresh-name counter — the variable under
test. Strict mode correctly rejects an unused fact, so `--draft` is the right mode and
not a weakening here.

READ THE PROBE NOTES BEFORE TRUSTING A PROBE NAME. Probes 2 and 3 were originally
named and described as counter tests and are NOT — they compare two different
theorems. Probes 4 and 5 are the ones that isolate the counter.

SCHEMA-HASH-DECODE-PLAN.md picks route A (re-derivation): "Do not store anything. On
demand, enumerate candidate instantiations, recompute `instanceHash` for each, and
report the ones that match." Its stated cost is "a scan over citation sites per query".

THAT UNDERSTATES THE COST, and the probes say by how much.

PROBE 1 — lambda-arg-is-counter-free.b4m. A schema instantiated at a LAMBDA argument
(`(fun k: Nat => add(k, ZERO) = k)`), twice, once after warm-up `fix` steps that bump
the proof's fresh-name counter. Result: ONE instance, one hash. The lambda's params
canonicalize BY POSITION in `hashTerm` (the `#` branch), so the counter does not reach
the hash. Good — and this is the case the plan's examples are all drawn from.

PROBE 2 — eigenvar-args-in-two-theorems.b4m (renamed 2026-10-18; it was called
`eigenvar-arg-depends-on-the-counter.b4m`, which is NOT what it measures). A schema with
a VALUE param, instantiated INSIDE a `fix` at an argument that MENTIONS the fix's
eigenvar, in each of two theorems, the second preceded by a warm-up `fix`:

    addZeroOnTheRight{adea7c551b3c7020}      <- in `usedEarly`
    addZeroOnTheRight{e78561b481fdd421}      <- in `usedLate`

Two hashes — but `usedEarly` and `usedLate` are TWO DIFFERENT THEOREMS, so these are two
genuinely different instantiations at two different binders, and two addresses is CORRECT.
The warm-up is a red herring: deleting it (PROBE 3) collapses them only because it also
makes the two proofs structurally identical.

PROBE 3 — eigenvar-arg-without-the-warmup.b4m. The same file with the warm-up deleted;
both theorems now hash to `adea7c551b3c7020`. Read together with PROBE 2 this LOOKS like
proof that the counter is the cause, and that reading is what the original text asserted.
It is wrong — see PROBES 4 and 5.

PROBES 4 and 5 — counter-shifts-a-binder-name.b4m and
counter-shifts-a-binder-name-no-warmup.b4m. These are the files that actually isolate the
counter: ONE theorem, so ONE binder, and — the part that makes the pair valid — the two
files are BYTE-ALIGNED. Deleting the warm-up would otherwise move every later byte and so
move the binder, which is exactly the confound that invalidated probes 2 and 3. So the
warm-up is replaced by a comment block of EXACTLY its byte count: both files are 1603 bytes
and `fix a` sits at offset 1408 in each, with the binder name at 1412. The ONLY difference is
how many fresh names are minted before the binder.

    $ 2b4m check --draft --trace-facts <each file> | grep addZeroOnTheRight

                               with warm-up        warm-up deleted
    counter scheme      e78561b481fdd421    adea7c551b3c7020   <- TWO addresses
    loc scheme (fixed)  5f947fd7a9ca55ea    5f947fd7a9ca55ea   <- one

That is the real defect and the fix, measured: the same written instantiation in the same
proof got two addresses depending only on what preceded it.

(The mint itself was also instrumented while diagnosing: `a#4` with the warm-up vs `a#2`
without, under the counter scheme; `a#1412` — the binder's source offset — under the fix.)

WHY. An eigenvar is `freshNamed`'d, so its name is `<prefix>#<counter>` where
`Prove.fresh_counter` counts every fresh name minted EARLIER IN THAT PROOF
(Prove.zig:284). `hashTerm`'s fvar case canonicalizes only lambda params (the `#`
branch, by position in `canon`); every OTHER fvar takes the `@` branch and hashes its
NAME BYTES (Schema.zig:120-127). An eigenvar is not a lambda param, so its counter
value is in the hash.

FIXED 2026-10-18 — `bindProofVar` now keys a USER binder's name to its source offset
(`freshNamedAt`), synthetic binders keeping the counter. See EIGENVAR-CANONICALIZATION.

WHAT THIS MEANS FOR ROUTE A. Re-deriving a hash from a citation site requires
reproducing the proof's fresh-counter state at that step — i.e. REPLAYING THE PROOF UP
TO THE INSTANTIATION, not elaborating the site in isolation. The plan's "walking proofs
for instantiation steps yields the candidate set directly" holds for the CANDIDATE SET
but not for the HASH: binding an argument needs a live `Elab` with scope, sort
resolution and the counter (Prove.bindSchemaArgs / bindLambdaArg, ~Prove.zig:2020-2105).

THREE WAYS FORWARD, and the choice is a real one:

  A'. RE-DERIVATION BY REPLAY. Run the prover again with a decode hook: at each
      instantiation, compute the hash and compare. Correct by construction, since it IS
      the same code path. Cost is one extra proving pass per query — acceptable on an
      error path, and the plan's own rationale ("slow is fine") covers it. No new data
      structure. This is the smallest honest version of route A.

  B. THE REVERSE SIDE TABLE the plan rejects. Its stated reason is the lock-discipline
      hazard around Context side tables ([[side-table-lock-discipline]]). That reason
      stands, but note the table would be written ONCE per instance at the naming site
      and read only by a query — not during proving — so it is not the shape that
      segfaulted. Worth re-weighing now that route A is known to need a replay.

  C. MAKE THE HASH COUNTER-FREE: canonicalize eigenvars the way lambda params already
      are. Then re-derivation works from the site alone, as the plan assumed. This is
      the most invasive but it fixes the underlying oddity — two identical
      instantiations currently mint TWO instances and prove the same thing twice, which
      is wasted work regardless of decoding. Note the hash's comment already records a
      PREVIOUS determinism bug of exactly this family (`cc800fe`, Indexes vs names), so
      this is the third iteration on the same question.

RECOMMENDATION: C, then A' becomes trivial. C is also the only one that makes the
duplicate-instance waste go away, and the hash's own doc comment says what it is for —
"it addresses a schema instance" — which two spellings of one instantiation violate.

NOT YET DISCUSSED WITH THE USER. The plan was approved on the understanding that
re-derivation is cheap and self-contained; probe 2 says it is not, so the choice above
should be theirs.

================================================================================
RESOLVED 2026-10-18 — C LANDED, IN A CORRECTED FORM; A' IS NO LONGER NEEDED
================================================================================

The recommendation above (C, then A') was taken, but C as stated there was WRONG and
breaking `std/` twice is what showed it. "Canonicalize eigenvars the way lambda params
already are" means keying them BY POSITION, and an eigenvar-keyed instance is not a
reusable fact: its statement mentions a variable bound inside ONE proof. Positional
canonicalization therefore collapses two proofs' distinct instances onto one address and
hands the second proof the first's statement. Both attempts are recorded in
EIGENVAR-CANONICALIZATION.

WHAT LANDED instead keeps the binder as identity and removes only the serial:
`bindProofVar` names a USER binder `<stem>#<source offset>` (`Prove.freshNamedAt`), with
SYNTHETIC binders still on the counter because `Accelerant.Builder.tok` stamps one shared
loc per generated proof.

CONSEQUENCE FOR THIS PLAN. The counter is out of the hash, so the premise of A' — "the
hash cannot be re-derived without replaying the proof" — no longer holds for the counter.
Route A as originally written (re-derivation from the citation site) is viable again: the
remaining inputs are the namespace, the schema name, and the argument terms, all readable
at the site. A' (replay) and B (reverse side table) are both unnecessary.

ONE CAVEAT, and it is a real one: the hash is now stable under proof-INTERNAL edits but
NOT under reformatting, since a binder's offset moves when `2b4m fmt` rewrites whitespace.
Nothing persists an instance address across runs (it keys FactKV in-process and appears in
`--trace-facts`), so this costs nothing today — but it does mean a decode feature must
re-derive against the CURRENT bytes of the file, never a stored hash from an earlier run.
